      "content": "Traditionalist theologians often present their views as the only correct, objective moral framework, grounding their claims in Scripture and history. They typically assume that their interpretation of biblical teachings and traditional values is the most coherent understanding of God's purpose.\n\nHowever, they often refrain from explicitly engaging with critical perspectives that celebrate the same changes as progress, progressive values, or personal liberties. Instead, they might frame their opponents as misunderstanding or rejecting God's design for human society. In formal writings, they tend to emphasize the stark contrast between their understanding of God's intention and the evolving norms and laws in modern society.\n\nThat being said, many traditionalist theologians are motivated by a deep-seated conviction that their views are intrinsically linked to the nature of God, human beings, and the external world. They often describe their stance as rooted in biblical, Catholic or theological tradition, suggesting that these traditional notions are fundamental to human flourishing and coherent personal expectations in general.",
